some paris airport workers voted to end  their protest after talks with  management showed signs of progress  staff have staged a number of walkouts  in the past month to demand better pay  and conditions they say they are  overworked and underpaid  inflation is soaring but our salaries  have not increased so what we have been  asking for is an extra 300 a month for  every airport worker  with a number of flights cancelled or  delayed in recent weeks some travellers  are taking precautions  we've come early in case we're stuck  here you have to plan for these things  in advance  we were aware of the strike so we  arrived earlier because we were worried  the post-pandemic mix of staff shortages  and inflation have led to similar  strikes in some other european countries  in spain cabin crew for some low-cost  airlines are planning walkouts over pay  this month while scandinavian airlines  has drastically cut its services because  of an ongoing pilot strike meanwhile  british airways has cancelled ten  thousand summer flights because of a  lack of workers  with borders reopening and covered  restrictions lifting demand for travel  is surging here in paris international  travelers are back in big numbers for  the first time in nearly two years  even if the airport workers demands are  met this expert says that staff  shortages are likely to remain a problem  for some time  airports here lost more than 10 000  workers from ground staff to pilots  during the pandemic on flights were cut  so there are huge shortages the problem  is that you can't hire these workers  from one day to another many need  training and they certainly won't be  operational in time for the summer  after being grounded for so long many  people are enjoying being able to jet  off again for work or pleasure but the  ongoing strikes and planned protests  suggest it could be a turbulent summer  for travellers  natasha butler al jazeera paris
 

